<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Min (Ten Kingdoms)</h1><p class="st-meta">909 CE – 945 CE · capital Changle (Fuzhou)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A kingdom in Fujian under the Wang family, which opened the province to overseas trade before tearing itself apart in dynastic feuds.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-chao">Wang Chao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-shenzhi">Wang Shenzhi</a>, leaders of a band of soldiers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>, conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> in the 890s. After Wang Chao's death in 898 Wang Shenzhi ruled from Changle (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a>), and in 909 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-liang">Later Liang</a> named him Prince of Min.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-shenzhi">Wang Shenzhi</a> governed frugally, lightened taxes, promoted schools and welcomed foreign merchants to the ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Quanzhou</a>, laying the base for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian's</a> later maritime prosperity. He was a generous patron of Buddhist monasteries. His successors were less able: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-yanjun">Wang Yanjun</a> proclaimed himself emperor in 933, and later rulers murdered each other in a series of coups.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 943 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-yanzheng">Wang Yanzheng</a> founded a rival state, Yin, at Jianzhou, and civil war followed. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-tang">Southern Tang</a> conquered most of Min in 945; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a> passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wuyue">Wuyue</a> in 947 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Quanzhou</a> to local commanders who submitted to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a> in 978.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/min-ten-kingdoms.jpg" alt="Min (Ten Kingdoms)" width="320" height="437"><figcaption>Image: Pavo Xie · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L.LIANG.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>909 CE – 945 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Changle (Fuzhou)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Wang Shenzhi, Wang Yanjun, Wang Yanxi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese, Min Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Min, Min Kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=927&amp;focus=min-ten-kingdoms" aria-label="Min (Ten Kingdoms) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7831 1220 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Min (Ten Kingdoms) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8151 1476L7855 1179M8151 1476L8056 1267"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7855 1179h0M8056 1267h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8151 1476h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8151 1476h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=927&amp;focus=min-ten-kingdoms">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

